/**

@page benchmarks_exahype2_euler_smooth_setup Convergence Tests (Euler Equations)

\tableofcontents

This benchmark checks that the numerical schemes of ExaHyPE 2 converge with
their formal order. It uses the Euler equations over @f$ [-0.1,0.1]^d @f$
(@f$ d=2,3 @f$), where the density follows one period of a sine wave along x,
while velocity @f$ u=1 @f$ (along x) and pressure @f$ p=1 @f$ are constant.
The exact solution is the initial density profile advected with the velocity,
i.e.

@f$ \rho(x,t) = 1 + 0.2 \sin \left( 2 \pi \frac{x - t + 0.1}{0.2} \right). @f$

By default, the boundary conditions prescribe this exact solution outside of
the domain. Pass --periodic for periodic boundary conditions instead.

The script smooth-convergence-test.py supports the following numerical schemes
(--scheme): Finite Volumes with a Rusanov solver and explicit Euler time stepping
(fv, @ref exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler), the same with optimistic time
stepping (fv-optimistic), and fourth-order Finite Differences with Runge-Kutta
time stepping of order one to four (rk1fd4, ..., rk4fd4, @ref exahype2.api.rkfd4).
The CFL constant is a property of the scheme and hard-coded per scheme in the script.


## Prepare benchmark

Configure and build Peano with

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
--enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

and ensure that the Python environment variables are set:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
export PYTHONPATH=<path-to-peano4-repository>/src
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## Run

The script smooth-convergence-test.py generates and builds the code for
one scheme and one mesh width. All PDE terms are passed to the solver as C++
snippets, so there is no user code to write. Build and run one executable per
mesh width, each in a directory of its own:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
for volume_size in 0.0075 0.0025 0.00083; do
  python3 smooth-convergence-test.py --scheme rk4fd4 --output-directory run-$volume_size --volume-size $volume_size
  (cd run-$volume_size && ./smooth-convergence-test)
done
python3 compute-errors.py run-0.0075 run-0.0025 run-0.00083
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

convergence-study.py does the same in one go and checks the order (it is part of the
integration test, skill exahype2:integration-test):

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
python3 convergence-study.py --scheme rk4fd4 --work-directory <directory-outside-of-the-repository>
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The three volume sizes yield 27, 81 and 243 grid cells per axis, as each mesh
cell hosts a patch of 9x9 grid cells. python3 smooth-convergence-test.py --help
lists further options: the task graph compiler, the C++ compiler, the number of
threads used to build (default: all cores) and to run, fixed time step sizes or three
dimensions.

compute-errors.py compares the density of the last snapshot of each run with
the exact solution, and prints the maximum and the root mean square error
plus the convergence orders between subsequent runs. The plot files hold
the patches' offsets with seven significant digits only. As this would
dominate the errors on the finer meshes, the script snaps the offsets onto
the regular mesh.


## Results

With the default settings, i.e. rk4fd4 (adaptive time step sizes with a CFL
constant of 0.5, no dissipation), Dirichlet boundary conditions and the end
time 0.05, we obtain in 2d:

| Grid cells per axis | Time steps | Max error of density | RMS error of density | Order (max) | Order (RMS) |
|--------------------:|-----------:|---------------------:|---------------------:|------------:|------------:|
| 27                  |         64 | 3.82e-05             | 2.10e-05             |             |             |
| 81                  |        190 | 4.77e-07             | 2.59e-07             | 3.99        | 4.00        |
| 243                 |        566 | 5.89e-09             | 3.18e-09             | 4.00        | 4.01        |

The errors decrease with the fourth power of the mesh width. As the time
step size is proportional to the mesh width, this confirms the order of the
Runge-Kutta scheme, too.

*/
